    "plan_strategy": "Single attempt: replace the linear baseline with a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt) sized for 12,967 training rows. House prices in this dataset are driven by strong non-linear, spatially-interacting structure (lat/long/zipcode crossed with sqft_living and grade) that a scaled ridge cannot express at all; a boosted tree ensemble builds those interactions internally, which is exactly the 'better ranking generator' the goal asks for. Configuration: hist_gbdt with learning_rate 0.06, max_iter 900, max_leaf_nodes 63, min_samples_leaf 15, l2_regularization 1.0, max_bins 255, early_stopping true with validation_fraction 0.1 and n_iter_no_change 30 so the 900-iteration budget is a ceiling rather than a commitment and the fit stops before it starts memorising the 5.3% high-price outlier tail. Preprocessing: median impute (the card reports zero missingness, so this is inert) and no scaling, since tree splits are scale-invariant; no missingness columns, since there is no missingness to encode. Full training set, no subsample — cost-3 model on ~13k rows and 21 numeric columns fits comfortably inside 600s and 2GB."
